re-resolved commit, the licence grant reproduced where required, an explicit answer to "who
originated the idea" separate from "whose licence covers the bytes", and a what-is/what-is-not
derived pair per donor. `pi-lab/NOTICE` itself states plainly that it is establishing a shape
for the *next* donor to follow rather than an established convention this repository already
had — this file is that next donor's record, for a disjoint, smaller set of derivations.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The provenance rule this file applies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Where a donor names a third party as the origin of an idea, **the provenance is the third
party, and the donor's licence grant does not launder it.** A permissive licence settles
whether text may be reused. It says nothing about who thought of the thing. Both questions
are answered separately below, for every donor, and a derived file's own header repeats the
answer where a reader of that file will see it. This rule is copied from `pi-lab/NOTICE`
(same author, MIT), which states it was itself written once for a 2026-07-30 harvest fan-in
recorded outside that repository, and treats it as durable house doctrine rather than
reinventing an equivalent.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The register, stated plainly: intra-family only, so far
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every derivation this file records below is **intra-family**: the donor is a sibling
repository under the same author (Baladithya Balamurugan, MIT throughout), and no sentence,
table, or worked example was carried across in any case — only ideas, re-expressed in this
repository's own prose and vocabulary. This matters for the provenance question specifically:

  * `pi-lab`'s own `house/loop-design` skill carries a *separate* file,
    `skills/house/loop-design/references/external-anchors-and-loop-shape.md`, that itself
    derives from a third party (`affaan-m/ECC`, MIT, whose own footer attributes the
    underlying ideas further upstream to Norbert Wiener, Anatoli, and Addy Osmani —
    `pi-lab/NOTICE`'s Donor 1). **That chain does not reach this repository.** The two files
    in this repository that draw on `pi-lab`'s loop-design material
    (`skills/agentic-sdlc/references/deep-work-loop.md`) cite only `pi-lab`'s main
    `house/loop-design/SKILL.md` body, which attributes its own central claim (a loop's stop
    condition must be externally adjudicated, never self-graded) to Anthropic's own published
    engineering posts (`vendor-doc` class throughout that section), not to the ECC-mediated
    Wiener/Anatoli/Osmani chain. Checked directly: `pi-lab/skills/house/loop-design/SKILL.md`
    itself never names Wiener, Anatoli, or Osmani; only its sibling `references/` file does,
    and this repository does not cite that sibling file anywhere (`rg -n
    'Wiener|Anatoli|Osmani|Nygard|MADR|Zdun|Zimmermann' skills/` returns no hits in this
    repository at the time of this pass).
  * `pi-lab`'s ADR-authoring skill separately derives from `adr/madr` and `adr/adr.github.io`
    (`pi-lab/NOTICE`'s Donor 2, crediting Michael Nygard and Zdun et al. upstream). This
    repository has no ADR-authoring skill or reference file at all (`rg -n
    'MADR|Nygard' skills/` returns no hits), so that chain also does not reach here.

So the honest statement is: **this repository's donor register is currently intra-family
only.** No third-party idea has yet traveled through a `pi-lab` intermediary into a file in
this repository. If that changes — if a future edit here draws on
`external-anchors-and-loop-shape.md`, the ADR template material, or any other file in
`pi-lab/NOTICE` whose own provenance is a third party — the upstream credit (Wiener, Anatoli,
Osmani, Nygard, or the Y-statement authors, as applicable) must be repeated here under the
`author-claim about a third party` evidence class exactly as `pi-lab/NOTICE` states it, never
